1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,355
Let's talk about getting genuinely better results — the handful of prompt patterns that measurably help a tool-using agent.

2
00:00:03,355 --> 00:00:06,000
I'll show before-and-after on camera so you can see the difference, not just take my word for it.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,278
Three that pay off immediately.

4
00:00:07,278 --> 00:00:09,876
Say what you don't want — “skip style comments, only real bugs.

5
00:00:09,876 --> 00:00:13,918
” Give it explicit permission to find nothing — that single line kills padded, low-signal answers.

6
00:00:13,918 --> 00:00:18,000
And ask it to show its evidence or quote the source — the best defense against confident invention.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,401
Three more about how you drive it.

8
00:00:19,401 --> 00:00:22,655
Ask for a plan before any multi-file work — cheaper to fix a plan than an edit.

9
00:00:22,655 --> 00:00:24,509
Work in small steps instead of one giant ask.

10
00:00:24,509 --> 00:00:29,000
And tell it what to do when stuck: ask you, or make an assumption and flag it — so it doesn't silently guess.

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:38,338
And what doesn't help, so you can stop wasting words on it: politeness, “you are a senior engineer” role-play framing, very long instructions, and — importantly — repeating an instruction it already ignored.

12
00:00:38,338 --> 00:00:41,000
None of these measurably improve a modern tool-using model.

13
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,239
That last one deserves a gotcha.

14
00:00:42,239 --> 00:00:48,320
Repeating an ignored instruction almost never works, because the problem usually isn't emphasis — it's that something else in the prompt is contradicting it.

15
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,000
Go back to the layering model from Episode 11 and find the conflict instead of shouting louder.

16
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,360
That's the whole beginner toolkit.

17
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:57,440
Next episode we put all of Track A together on a real codebase — the first proper end-to-end use case.

18
00:00:57,440 --> 00:00:58,000
See you there.

